        <div class="tool-seo">
          <h2>What Is Trail in Motorcycle Geometry?</h2>
          <p>Trail is the horizontal distance between where the front wheel contacts the ground and where the steering axis (extended through the fork tubes) meets the ground. It is the single most important geometry dimension for predicting a motorcycle's self-centring behaviour and handling feel.</p>
          <h2>How Rake and Fork Offset Interact</h2>
          <p>Rake (also called caster angle) is the angle of the steering axis from vertical. A steeper rake increases trail, making the bike feel more stable but slower to turn. Fork offset counteracts this: a larger offset reduces trail, making the steering quicker and lighter. Sportbikes use a combination of steep rake and large offset to achieve quick handling without instability; cruisers use shallow rake and minimal offset for high-speed stability and classic proportions.</p>
          <h2>Trail Targets by Riding Style</h2>
          <p>60–85mm trail is typical of track and race-biased machines — extremely responsive but requiring active rider input. 85–110mm covers the bulk of sport and naked bikes where handling and stability are balanced. 110–130mm suits touring machines that prioritise high-speed stability over steering agility. Above 130mm is the domain of cruisers, choppers, and long-haul bikes where straight-line feel is paramount.</p>
